1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,276
An exit is a transaction that may
create a path to payment, such as

2
00:00:04,276 --> 00:00:06,637
an acquisition or public listing,
but

3
00:00:06,637 --> 00:00:09,062
there is no guarantee one will
happen.

4
00:00:09,062 --> 00:00:13,070
If a company is sold, the money
does not necessarily flow to

5
00:00:13,070 --> 00:00:15,875
every investor in proportion to
ownership.

6
00:00:15,875 --> 00:00:20,152
Debt, transaction costs, and
preferred-stock rights may be

7
00:00:20,152 --> 00:00:21,775
paid or applied first.

8
00:00:21,775 --> 00:00:25,541
A liquidation preference can give
certain preferred investors

9
00:00:25,541 --> 00:00:27,023
priority up to a defined

10
00:00:27,023 --> 00:00:29,862
amount before common holders
receive proceeds.

11
00:00:29,862 --> 00:00:34,694
In a simplified example, preferred
investors put in $2 million with a

12
00:00:34,694 --> 00:00:37,425
one-times non-participating
preference.

13
00:00:37,425 --> 00:00:41,760
If the company sells for $1.5
million and no higher-priority

14
00:00:41,760 --> 00:00:46,022
claims exist, those investors
could take the available $1.5

15
00:00:46,022 --> 00:00:49,562
million, leaving nothing for
common shareholders.

16
00:00:49,562 --> 00:00:54,639
At a $12 million sale, they might
choose between their $2 million

17
00:00:54,639 --> 00:00:57,217
preference and converting to
20%,

18
00:00:57,217 --> 00:01:00,262
or $2.4 million, under the
assumptions.

19
00:01:00,262 --> 00:01:04,338
Actual outcomes depend on the
documents and waterfall.

20
00:01:04,338 --> 00:01:08,808
Read the security class and sale
terms; a headline valuation does

21
00:01:08,808 --> 00:01:11,352
not show what each investor
receives.

